From 09adda8f338591b31b3302b0e4b990ea751ccf14 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: port <port@localhost>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 09:10:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08/14] wgpu: draw BitmapData.draw in place when it can, cap
 the offscreen pool

render_offscreen copies the target texture into a pooled frame buffer of the
same size, draws there and copies the result back. The pool keeps one frame
buffer per size until the end of the frame, and the command encoder keeps
them alive until it is submitted. Games that build a level cache out of
dozens of large BitmapData in one tick (one game's Level
4: 65 tiles, 76 MB of pooled frame buffers) briefly doubled the cache memory
on the GPU.

* In place (RUFFLE_DRAW_IN_PLACE=0 restores the old path): at one sample
  per pixel, when the commands do not sample the target itself, they are
  drawn straight into the bitmap texture with the new
  RenderTargetMode::ExistingKeep (load, no clear). The check walks the
  command list: bitmaps, Stage3D outputs, shape bitmap fills (meshes now
  keep the handles of the bitmaps their fills sample), masks and blend
  lists. Multisampled quality and self-sampling draws keep the old path.
* Pool cap (RUFFLE_DRAW_POOL_MB, default 16, 0 = off): once the remaining
  pooled draws since the last trim add up to that many MB, the work is
  submitted and the offscreen pool emptied, so their frame buffers are freed
  before the frame ends.

The result of a draw is the same either way. Measured with the other memory
changes on the PC: World 3 Level 4 peak 546 -> 473 MB, World 2 Level 1
351 -> 326 MB.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
